  "issues": [
    {
      "number": 3,
      "id": "I_kwDOSCpQkc8AAAABAK7gnw",
      "title": "Only send the most recent SCONE_ECHO",
      "url": "https://github.com/martinduke/scone-echo/issues/3",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "ianswett",
      "authorAssociation": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "Similar to MAX_DATA frames in QUIC, I think it makes sense to stop sending old SCONE_ECHO values once there's a newer value available from a more recent SCONE packet.\n\nThis also avoids any potential issues with buffering SCONE_ECHO frames.",
      "createdAt": "2026-04-22T02:49:29Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-04-28T14:21:52Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-04-28T14:21:52Z",
      "comments": [
        {
          "author": "martinduke",
          "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
          "body": "MT suggested only sending it on update, so maybe just send on update, and then only the most recent update?",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-23T02:02:22Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-23T02:02:22Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "LPardue",
          "authorAssociation": "NONE",
          "body": "Either way, the MAX_DATA model is pretty good and for retransmissions you would want to send the most recent sample ",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-23T02:27:55Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-23T02:27:55Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "martinduke",
          "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
          "body": "Opened #6 for MT's suggestion, which is a bit more aggressive than what's necessary to avoid DoS follies.",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-28T03:27:36Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-28T03:27:36Z"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 4,
      "id": "I_kwDOSCpQkc8AAAABARFARQ",
      "title": "Privacy: Easier to harvest bandwidth readings",
      "url": "https://github.com/martinduke/scone-echo/issues/4",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "martinduke",
      "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "With existing SCONE, an attacker can send scone_supported and thus (sometimes) receive SCONE packets that give it bandwidth advice from other points in the internet to itself, whether or not it actually has a supporting application. However, the peer must be configured to send SCONE.\n\nWith SCONE_ECHO, it is also possible to measure the other direction: with widely deployed SCONE_ECHO senders, an attacker can send SCONE packets and get readings outbound, again without any supporting application at either end.\n\nSo the direction of harvesting changes, and it also *easier* than it is in SCONE.",
      "createdAt": "2026-04-23T01:52:50Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-04-28T15:47:59Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-04-28T15:47:59Z",
      "comments": []
    },
    {
      "number": 6,
      "id": "I_kwDOSCpQkc8AAAABArTqHw",
      "title": "ECHO only on update?",
      "url": "https://github.com/martinduke/scone-echo/issues/6",
      "state": "OPEN",
      "author": "martinduke",
      "authorAssociation": "OWNER",
      "assignees": [],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "There is one SCONE_ECHO frame per SCONE packet.\n\nInstead, the receiver could only send SCONE_ECHO when the value changes. My instinct is to keep the ECHO logic as dumb as possible (maybe the repeat signal is useful?), and am concerned about reordering and other effects. But I don't feel strongly about it.",
      "createdAt": "2026-04-28T03:26:52Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-04-28T03:26:52Z",
      "closedAt": null,
      "comments": []
    }
  ],
